.\" make.1 -- maux manual page for make(1), src/bin/make.s and its
.\" four body modules (makeread.s, makemac.s, makeinf.s, makerun.s).
.\" ORIGINAL to maux: no V7 or BSD text is reproduced here, so no
.\" third-party notice applies (docs/MAN.md sec 7).
.\" Rendered by make man-pages and staged as /usr/man/MAKE.
.TH MAKE 1 "September 18, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
make \- keep files up to date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B make
[
.B \-eikNnpqrSst
] [
.B \-f
.I makefile
] [
.I name=value
] ... [
.I target
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Make
reads a makefile of rules and, for each
.I target
named on the command line, brings that target's prerequisites up to
date and then runs the target's recipe if any prerequisite is newer
than the target or the target does not exist.
With no
.I target
operand it builds the first rule in the file whose target does not
begin with a period.
.PP
A makefile is made of four kinds of line:
.TP
.B "NAME = value"
A macro definition. Later,
.BR $(NAME) ,
.B ${NAME}
or, for a one-character name,
.BR $N ,
expands to the value.
.B $$
is a literal dollar sign, and an undefined macro expands to nothing.
A macro given on the command line overrides one in the makefile;
an exported environment variable is a macro too, and
.B \-e
makes the environment win over the makefile.
.TP
.B "target ... : prerequisite ... [; command]"
A rule. Several targets on one line make several rules sharing the
prerequisites and the recipe.
.TP
.B "<tab>command"
A recipe line for the rule above it. A leading
.B @
suppresses the echo, a leading
.B \-
ignores a nonzero exit, and a leading
.B +
runs the line even under
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B "# comment"
A comment, to the end of the line. Comments are recognised on macro
and rule lines only, never inside a recipe: with no shell in the way
(see BUGS) a recipe line IS the command.
.PP
The makefile may be in either text convention: maux text (high bit set,
tab $89, lines ended by $8D) or ProDOS-standard text (low ASCII, tab $09,
lines ended by $0D); both build the same bytes.
.PP
A line ending in
.B \e
is continued on the next; the join is one blank and the continuation's
own indentation is dropped.
.SS Inference rules
A target with no rule of its own can be made by an
.I inference rule.
.B .SUFFIXES
lists the suffixes to try, in order; a second
.B .SUFFIXES
adds to the list and an empty one clears it. A rule whose target is
two suffixes written together says how to make the second from the
first, so with
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&.SUFFIXES: .o .c
\&.c.o:
	cc \-c $<
.fi
.RE
.PP
the target
.B prog.o
is made from
.B prog.c
if that file exists or is itself a target.
There are NO built-in suffixes or rules: this system has no compiler
to write one for, so a makefile declares its own.
.PP
A suffix here need not begin with a period.
.I Make
joins the pair to name the rule and never takes it apart again, so
.B ".SUFFIXES: R .S"
with a rule
.B .SR:
makes
.B CATMR
from
.BR CATM.S ,
which is what maux's own self-rebuild uses.
.SS Internal macros
Inside a recipe:
.TP
.B $@
the target.
.TP
.B $<
the prerequisite an inference rule was chosen for; in an ordinary
rule, the first prerequisite.
.TP
.B $*
the target with its suffix removed.
.TP
.B $?
the prerequisites that are newer than the target. It is recomputed
when it is used, so it costs one
.BR stat (2)
per prerequisite.
.SS Special targets
.B .SILENT
and
.B .IGNORE
do what
.B \-s
and
.B \-i
do.
.B .DEFAULT
gives a recipe for any target that has no rule and no file.
.B .PRECIOUS
and
.B .POSIX
are accepted and do nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-e
Environment variables override macros set in the makefile.
.TP
.BI \-f " makefile"
Read
.I makefile
instead of MAKEFILE. The name may be attached (\fB\-f\fIname\fR).
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore the exit status of every command.
.TP
.B \-k
On a failing command, abandon that target but carry on with targets
that do not depend on it.
.TP
.B \-n
Write the commands that would run; run none of them. A line beginning
.B @
is still written.
.TP
.B \-p
Write the macro definitions and the target descriptions.
.TP
.B \-q
Say nothing and run nothing; exit 1 if any named target is out of
date.
.TP
.B \-r
Clear the initial suffix list. There is no built-in list, so this
does nothing and is accepted for portability.
.TP
.B \-S
Undo a previous
.BR \-k .
.TP
.B \-s
Do not echo commands.
.TP
.B \-t
Bring each target up to date by dating it, without running its
recipe.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B MAKEFILE
read when
.B \-f
is not given.
.TP
.B /usr/man/MAKE
this page.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A recipe's command word is located the way sh(1) locates one: looked
up on PATH, except that a word containing a slash is a path and is not
searched. Unlike sh(1), make has no retry of a file that begins with
#!, and a leading
.B \-
carries on after a command that is not found but not after one that
cannot be run. A command that cannot
be found stops make with
.B make: NAME: not found
(a leading
.B \-
or
.B \-i
carries on).
.PP
Exit 0 if every target was already up to date or was made, 1 on any
failure (or, under
.BR \-q ,
if a target is out of date), 2 on a usage error.
.SH BUGS
There is no shell between
.I make
and the command. A recipe line is split on blanks and the program is
spawned directly, so a recipe has no pipes, no redirection, no
globbing, and no
.BR cd .
.PP
File times have one-minute resolution, so a target rebuilt within a
minute of its source's last write compares equal to it and is judged
up to date.
.B \-t
and
.BR touch (1)
are the way out; there is no
.B \-B
to force a build.
.PP
There is no parallelism, no
.BR include ,
no
.B ::
rules, and
.B "\-f \-"
cannot read a makefile from the standard input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nas (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR touch (1)
